# Line-ending policy for Zohara OS.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# This repo is developed on Windows (`core.autocrlf=true` on the dev machine) but every file in
# zohara-profile/airootfs/ ships to a Linux system, and the build scripts are executed by bash --
# inside Docker, and inside the mkarchiso chroot. Without this file, any `git checkout` / `git pull`
# / `git stash` on Windows rewrites those files with CRLF in the working tree, and
# scripts/build-iso-wsl.sh then rsyncs that working tree into the Linux build clone. The results are
# not subtle:
#
#   * customize_airootfs.sh gets `set -e\r`; bash reports errors on lines that look fine, and the
#     `cat > ... << 'EOF'` heredocs stop terminating because the terminator becomes `EOF\r`. With
#     `set -e` at the top of that script, mkarchiso aborts and no ISO is produced.
#   * Heredocs that *do* survive write CRLF into the generated /etc files (os-release, sddm confs,
#     pacman.conf), which systemd and pacman parse inconsistently.
#   * systemd unit files and .desktop entries with trailing CR silently fail to match values.
#
# `.gitattributes` takes precedence over `core.autocrlf`, so declaring eol=lf here makes checkouts
# correct on every platform without each developer having to configure their client.
#
# The committed blobs were verified clean (0 of 101 shell/unit/conf blobs contained CR as of
# 2026-08-24), so this file locks in the current state rather than fixing existing damage. If a CRLF
# blob ever does get committed, `git add --renormalize .` rewrites them to LF.

# Everything is LF by default: let git detect text vs binary, and never emit CRLF for text.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only helper scripts keep CRLF -- they are never run on Linux, and this preserves the
# behaviour they already have under core.autocrlf=true.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary assets: never apply end-of-line conversion or try to diff them as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico  binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.gz   binary
*.xz   binary
*.zst  binary
*.iso  binary
*.img  binary
*.pkg.tar.zst binary

# Cargo lockfiles are generated; keep them out of diff noise but still merge-resolvable.
Cargo.lock -diff
